{"meta": {"limit": 100, "next": null, "offset": 0, "previous": null, "total_count": 33}, "objects": [{"abstract": "   The Transport Layer Security (TLS) master secret is not\n   cryptographically bound to important session parameters such as the\n   client and server identities.  Consequently, it is possible for an\n   active attacker to set up two sessions, one with a client and another\n   with a server such that the master secrets on the two sessions are\n   the same.  Thereafter, any mechanism that relies on the master secret\n   for authentication, including renegotiation after session resumption,\n   becomes vulnerable to a man-in-the-middle attack, where the attacker\n   can simply forward messages back and forth between the client and\n   server.  This specification defines a TLS extension that contextually\n   binds the master secret to a log of the full handshake that computes\n   it, thus preventing such attacks.\n", "ad": null, "doc": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-tls-session-hash/", "expires": "2015-02-20T23:14:51Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1326/", "id": 62768, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-tls-session-hash", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/dochistory/62768/", "rev": "00", "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/nomcom-chair@ietf.org/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "tags": [], "time": "2014-08-19T22:14:51Z", "title": "Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Hash and Extended Master Secret Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Transport Layer Security (TLS) master secret is not\n   cryptographically bound to important session parameters such as the\n   client and server identities.  Consequently, it is possible for an\n   active attacker to set up two sessions, one with a client and another\n   with a server, such that the master secrets on the two sessions are\n   the same.  Thereafter, any mechanism that relies on the master secret\n   for authentication, including session resumption, becomes vulnerable\n   to a man-in-the-middle attack, where the attacker can simply forward\n   messages back and forth between the client and server.  This\n   specification defines a TLS extension that contextually binds the\n   master secret to a log of the full handshake that computes it, thus\n   preventing such attacks.\n", "ad": null, "doc": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-tls-session-hash/", "expires": "2015-02-23T14:47:10Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1326/", "id": 64688, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-tls-session-hash", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 8, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/dochistory/64688/", "rev": "01", "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": "/api/v1/person/email/nomcom-chair@ietf.org/", "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "tags": [], "time": "2014-08-22T13:47:10Z", "title": "Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Hash and Extended Master Secret Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Transport Layer Security (TLS) master secret is not\n   cryptographically bound to important session parameters such as the\n   client and server identities.  Consequently, it is possible for an\n   active attacker to set up two sessions, one with a client and another\n   with a server, such that the master secrets on the two sessions are\n   the same.  Thereafter, any mechanism that relies on the master secret\n   for authentication, including session resumption, becomes vulnerable\n   to a man-in-the-middle attack, where the attacker can simply forward\n   messages back and forth between the client and server.  This\n   specification defines a TLS extension that contextually binds the\n   master secret to a log of the full handshake that computes it, thus\n   preventing such attacks.\n", "ad": null, "doc": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-tls-session-hash/", "expires": "2015-04-09T13:16:23Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1326/", "id": 67382, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-ietf-tls-session-hash", "note": "", "notify": "", "pages": 9, "resource_uri": "/api/v1/doc/dochistory/67382/", "rev": "02", "rfc_number": null, "shepherd": null, "states": ["/api/v1/doc/state/1/", "/api/v1/doc/state/150/", "/api/v1/doc/state/38/"], "std_level": null, "stream": "/api/v1/name/streamname/ietf/", "tags": [], "time": "2014-10-06T13:16:23Z", "title": "Transport Layer Security (TLS) Session Hash and Extended Master Secret Extension", "type": "/api/v1/name/doctypename/draft/", "uploaded_filename": "", "words": null}, {"abstract": "   The Transport Layer Security (TLS) master secret is not\n   cryptographically bound to important session parameters.\n   Consequently, it is possible for an active attacker to set up two\n   sessions, one with a client and another with a server, such that the\n   master secrets on the two sessions are the same.  Thereafter, any\n   mechanism that relies on the master secret for authentication,\n   including session resumption, becomes vulnerable to a man-in-the-\n   middle attack, where the attacker can simply forward messages back\n   and forth between the client and server.  This specification defines\n   a TLS extension that contextually binds the master secret to a log of\n   the full handshake that computes it, thus preventing such attacks.\n", "ad": null, "doc": "/api/v1/doc/document/draft-ietf-tls-session-hash/", "expires": "2015-05-16T07:59:58Z", "external_url": "", "group": "/api/v1/group/group/1326/", "id": 67918, "intended_std_level": null, "keywords": "[]", "name": "draft-iet